`test-normalize-2.R` started failing because of this sf commit: https://github.com/r-spatial/sf/commit/8c3533e418c177f81d6bcd9d518c9994e719c3fc

The code below is a standalone reprex. Basically, the `st_as_sf` call near the end results in the holes' lat/lng vectors being "shifted" (same lat/lng points, same order, but starting with a different one).

One possibility for fixing this is to implement a comparison function that knows to disregard the reordering.

```r
library(sp)
library(sf)

create_square <- function(width = 2, lng = 0, lat = 0, hole = FALSE, type = Polygon) {
  lngs <- c(lng - width / 2, lng + width / 2, lng + width / 2, lng - width / 2)
  lats <- c(lat + width / 2, lat + width / 2, lat - width / 2, lat - width / 2)
  
  if (hole) {
    lngs <- rev(lngs)
    lats <- rev(lats)
  }
  
  if (hole) {
    type(cbind(lng = lngs, lat = lats), hole = hole)
  } else {
    type(cbind(lng = lngs, lat = lats))
  }
}

spolys <- SpatialPolygons(list(
  Polygons(list(
    create_square(),
    create_square(, 5, 5),
    create_square(1, hole = TRUE),
    create_square(1, 5, 5, hole = TRUE),
    create_square(0.4, 4.25, 4.25, hole = TRUE)
  ), "A")
))

stspolys <- st_as_sf(spolys)

waldo::compare(polygonData(spolys), polygonData(stspolys))
```

When clicking on a shape both a `map_shape_click `and a `map_click ` event are fired. I.e. the click is not consumed by the `map_shape_click`. 
I would expect the click event to be consumed by the `map_shape_click`. If there is no observer for this event then it might be beneficial to fire a `map_click ` event instead.
